26. Measuring Development: GDP, HDI, GDI, MPI and Quality of Life Indices
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: II | Topic: Measurement of development: conventional, HDI, GDI, MPI and other quality of life indices
Introduction
How do we compare development across countries? Early measures used income alone; later work argued that development includes health, education and freedom, leading to composite indices.
Conventional measures
GDP, GNI and per capita income, using PPP for comparability. Strengths: simple, available. Weaknesses: ignores distribution, non-market activity, environmental damage and quality of life, and says nothing on health and education. Other conventional measures: growth rate, structural change (share of industry), poverty headcount and Gini coefficient.
Human Development Index (HDI)
Introduced by UNDP (1990), inspired by Mahbub ul Haq and Amartya Sen. Three dimensions:
1. Health: life expectancy at birth.
2. Education: expected years of schooling and mean years of schooling.
3. Standard of living: GNI per capita (PPP, log).
Each dimension is converted to an index between 0 and 1 using minimum and maximum values; HDI is the geometric mean of the three (since 2010). Countries are classed as very high, high, medium or low human development. Criticisms: ignores inequality, environment and political freedom; the weights are arbitrary and the components are correlated with income.
Related indices
• Inequality-adjusted HDI (IHDI): discounts HDI for inequality.
• Gender Development Index (GDI): ratio of female to male HDI, showing gender gaps in the three dimensions.
• Gender Inequality Index (GII): reproductive health, empowerment and labour market participation.
• Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI): developed by Alkire and Foster (OPHI/UNDP, from 2010). Measures deprivations in 10 indicators across health (nutrition, child mortality), education (years of schooling, attendance) and living standards (cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water, electricity, housing, assets). A person is poor if deprived in at least one-third of weighted indicators. MPI = headcount ratio (H) x intensity (A). India's NITI Aayog publishes a national MPI.
• Others: Physical Quality of Life Index (Morris, 1979), Happiness Index, Planetary-pressures-adjusted HDI, Human Poverty Index (replaced by MPI), Genuine Savings.
Conclusion
No single measure captures development. GDP remains useful but should be supplemented by composite indices such as HDI and MPI that reflect the multidimensional nature of well-being.